<!-- RestoreRow — "Restore purchases", wired to the real thing on both lanes, and honest
     about the one it cannot reach.

     APPLE'S RULE (3.1.1): "you should make sure you have a restore mechanism for any
     restorable in-app purchases", and a subscription sign-up surface must let a current
     subscriber sign in OR restore. Both halves are real: <AuthSeam> is the sign-in, and this
     row is the restore.

     ══ RESTORE IS ONE VERB OVER TWO MECHANISMS, AND THE SPLIT IS ARCHITECTURAL ═══════════
     There is no browser twin for an app store — the store module's own manifest says so in
     as many words, and the reason is that StoreKit 2 and Play Billing are device-side OS
     services with no JS binding. So the verb "restore" resolves to a different question per
     lane, and this row runs whichever ones its build can ask:

       WEB      POST /store/restore         — ask Stripe about orders this backend opened
                                              for this subject and never granted.
       NATIVE   POST /store/restore/native  — ask RevenueCat what the store says this
                                              subject owns, and grant what is ungranted.
                PLUS the Stripe sweep, because a viewer who bought on the web storefront and
                came back on their phone has an unsettled order on the same account, and the
                phone is where they are asking.

     Neither route takes an argument naming the purchase, and that is deliberate: the client
     is never the authority on what was bought. `auth="required"` verifies a TOKEN — it does
     not ask for a human behind it — so the caller id is read server-side off a row the
     caller owns, and an anonymous session satisfies it exactly as a signed-in one does.

     ══ WHAT IT RECOVERS, AND WHAT NO IMPLEMENTATION COULD ════════════════════════════════
     RECOVERS, on either lane: money that was TAKEN and never granted — the app was killed,
     offline, or the settle call was lost between the provider confirming and the coins
     landing. That is the failure a restore button is actually for, and it is otherwise
     unreachable by the person it happened to.

     ALREADY TRUE WITHOUT IT: VIP and every unlocked episode are server-side, account-scoped
     rows. A reinstall or a second device that reaches the same subject has them, with
     nothing to restore. The resting line says so rather than implying a transfer happened.

     CANNOT, and this is a product truth rather than a gap: a purchase made under a DIFFERENT
     subject. Apple keys a receipt to an Apple ID and Google keys a purchase token to a
     Google account — per-account, never per-device — so the platform really can recognise a
     returning buyer with no app login. But every product here is a CONSUMABLE (coin packs by
     nature; the VIP pass by design), the platforms do not restore consumables, and replaying
     one would be wrong even where RevenueCat still remembers it: a consumable was already
     DELIVERED. Someone who bought 1,000 coins and spent 400 holds a balance of 600, not a
     1,000-coin receipt, so re-granting mints fresh coins on every reinstall.
     `unique (intent)` refuses that by design. A reinstall is therefore an IDENTITY problem —
     the coins are safe on the subject that bought them and the job is to reach that subject
     — which is why the sign-in offer sits beside this control instead of in front of it.

     ══ THE ONE THING THIS BUILD CANNOT DO, NAMED (Article 7) ═════════════════════════════
     The founder's requirement is that restore work with no login, because most of this
     product's revenue comes from viewers who never make an account. The SERVER half is built
     for exactly that: both routes need a subject, not a human. The CLIENT half is missing —
     <AuthSeam> `publishGuest` publishes `{ ok: false, headers: {} }`, so a guest carries no
     token at all and both routes would 401. Rather than fire a call that cannot succeed,
     this row says which half is missing and offers the sign-in that works today. The seam is
     one action in <AuthSeam> (mint a durable anonymous subject and publish it as a session);
     it is named here, in the README deploy checklist, and in PLAN.md §6.86.

     THIS ROW USED TO BE A LIE BY OMISSION, twice over. On /vip it called `wallet.refresh()`
     and claimed restoring "re-reads the account you are signed in to". Then it grew a real
     call and a header comment declaring restore anonymous — while its button still read
     "Sign in" and performed a restore anyway, so the label and the action disagreed. -->
<vstack spacing="0" alignItems="stretch">
  <head>
    <attribute as="variant" default="'row'"/>   <!-- row | compact | link | pill | ceiling -->

    <event as="restored" payload="coins"/>
    <!-- the PILL keeps no result line of its own (a nav-row control has nowhere to print one),
         so every outcome is also raised as an event and the host toasts it where its other
         messages land -->
    <event as="noted" payload="message"/>

    <api as="restoreCall" url="/store/restore" method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>
    <!-- the NATIVE sweep. Same trust shape, different question — server/store.dsx
         restoreNative asks RevenueCat rather than Stripe. -->
    <api as="nativeRestoreCall" url="/store/restore/native" method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>

    <variable as="busy">return false</variable>
    <variable as="result">return ''</variable>

    <variable as="signedIn" computed="true">return global.auth.ok == true</variable>
    <!-- CAPABILITY BEFORE PLATFORM (AGENTS.md, the <AdGate> precedent): a build that HAS the
         store module takes it wherever it runs, and only then does `os` decide. `blocked` is
         a native build with no store module — which is every native build today, because
         configuring Core/Store aborts the compile inside the module itself. -->
    <variable as="lane" computed="true">
      if (has('store')) { return 'native' }
      if (os == 'web') { return 'stripe' }
      return 'blocked'
    </variable>
    <variable as="line" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return 'Checking your purchases…' }
      if (dsx.variable.result != '') { return dsx.variable.result }
      if (dsx.variable.signedIn == false) {
        return 'Restoring needs a session this build does not mint for guests yet, so sign in to reach the purchases on your account. The routes behind this button never ask who you are — see RestoreRow.dsx.'
      }
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'native') {
        return 'VIP and every unlocked episode are held on your account, so they are already here. This asks the store what you have bought and applies anything that was paid for and never landed.'
      }
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'blocked') {
        return 'This build cannot ask the App Store or Google Play anything — it ships without the in-app-purchase module. What it can still check is a payment taken on the web storefront and never applied to your account.'
      }
      return 'VIP and every unlocked episode are held on your account, so they are already here. This checks for a payment that was taken but never applied.'
    </variable>
    <!-- THE SECOND LINE IS THE CEILING, per lane, and it is always shown — a limit a viewer
         only discovers after tapping is a limit the UI hid from them. -->
    <variable as="ceiling" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'stripe') {
        return 'A purchase made inside the iOS or Android app is restored in that app, by the store that took the payment — a browser cannot read an App Store or Play receipt.'
      }
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'blocked') {
        return 'Coin packs and passes are in-app-purchase products, so this build does not sell them here at all — see the note on the Store screen.'
      }
      return 'Coins you have already spent are not granted again: a coin pack is a consumable, so its value is delivered once. If you reinstalled, your balance is on the account that bought it — signing in is what reaches it.'
    </variable>
    <variable as="btnLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return 'Checking…' }
      return dsx.variable.signedIn ? 'Restore' : 'Sign in'
    </variable>
    <!-- the pill's word, through localize() so the table reaches a computed (scripts/strings.mjs) -->
    <variable as="pillLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return localize('Checking…') }
      if (dsx.variable.signedIn) { return localize('Restore') }
      return localize('Sign in')
    </variable>

    <!-- THE EMIT SHELL (Components/parts/Analytics.dsx). Four lines, and they are copied
         rather than shared because a `<functions global="true">` body is the EXPRESSION
         tier: dsx.log, dsx.module.* and a global write are all silent no-ops there, measured
         (PLAN.md §6.94). The POLICY is not copied — analyticsBlocked and analyticsSink are
         pure expressions and live in that one file. -->
    <action as="track" inputs="name, props">
      if (analyticsBlocked()) { return }
      if (analyticsSink() != '') { dsx.module.posthog.capture({ event: name, properties: props }) }
      else { dsx.log('funnel', name, props) }
    </action>

    <action as="tapped">
      // NOT A GATE, A NAMED GAP. The server would take an anonymous caller happily; this
      // build has no anonymous session to be one (see the header). Firing a call that can
      // only 401 would be a control that promises something it does not do, so the row says
      // which half is missing and sends the viewer at the sign-in that works today.
      if (dsx.variable.signedIn == false) {
        dsx.module.route.push({ path: '/auth/signin' })
        return
      }
      dsx.action.restore()
    </action>

    <action as="restore">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return }
      dsx.variable.busy = true
      dsx.variable.result = ''
      dsx.action.track({ name: 'restore', props: { phase: 'started', lane: dsx.variable.lane } })

      // ── THE NATIVE SWEEP FIRST, where the build can ask ──────────────────────────────
      // Two calls rather than one, because they ask different providers about different
      // purchases and a viewer can legitimately have both: a coin pack bought in the app,
      // and an order opened on the web storefront that never settled. `await` is a STATEMENT
      // and never a ternary branch (AGENTS.md, PLAN.md 6.31), so this is spelled long.
      let checked = 0
      let restored = 0
      let coins = 0
      let bonus = 0
      let vipDays = 0
      let more = false
      let failure = ''
      if (dsx.variable.lane == 'native') {
        const n = await nativeRestoreCall.send({})
        if (n.ok) {
          checked = checked + n.data.checked
          restored = restored + n.data.restored
          coins = coins + n.data.coins
          bonus = bonus + n.data.bonus
          vipDays = vipDays + n.data.vipDays
          if (n.data.done == false) { more = true }
        } else {
          // a declared rejection carries the words the server chose at r.data.message;
          // r.error is transport only
          failure = n.data != null && n.data.message != null ? n.data.message : ''
        }
      }

      const r = await restoreCall.send({})
      if (r.ok) {
        checked = checked + r.data.checked
        restored = restored + r.data.restored
        coins = coins + r.data.coins
        bonus = bonus + r.data.bonus
        vipDays = vipDays + r.data.vipDays
        // BOTH sweeps are bounded (twenty Stripe reads, five native grants), so both can
        // come back unfinished and either one unfinished means tap again
        if (r.data.done == false) { more = true }
      } else {
        const why = r.data != null && r.data.message != null ? r.data.message : ''
        if (failure == '') { failure = why }
      }
      dsx.variable.busy = false

      // A FAILURE ON ONE LANE DOES NOT ERASE A SUCCESS ON THE OTHER. Report what landed
      // first, then say what could not be checked — the opposite order loses the money.
      if (restored == 0 && failure != '') {
        dsx.variable.result = failure
        dsx.event('noted', { message: failure })
        return
      }
      dsx.action.track({ name: 'restore', props: { phase: 'done', lane: dsx.variable.lane, checked: checked, restored: restored } })
      if (restored == 0) {
        if (checked == 0) {
          dsx.variable.result = 'Nothing to restore — every purchase on this account is already applied.'
        } else {
          dsx.variable.result = 'Checked ' + checked + ' pending purchase(s): none had completed payment, so nothing was applied.'
        }
        dsx.event('noted', { message: dsx.variable.result })
        return
      }
      let got = ''
      if (coins > 0) { got = coins + ' coins' }
      if (bonus > 0) { got = got == '' ? bonus + ' bonus coins' : got + ' and ' + bonus + ' bonus coins' }
      if (vipDays > 0) { got = got == '' ? vipDays + ' VIP days' : got + ' and ' + vipDays + ' VIP days' }
      let said = 'Restored ' + restored + ' purchase(s): ' + got + ' are back on your account.'
      // RESUMABLE, AND THE VIEWER IS TOLD. restoreNative grants at most five per call
      // (ACTION_CALL_CAP); a half-finished restore that reports itself as finished is how
      // someone stops asking for money they are still owed.
      if (more) { said = said + ' More are still waiting — tap Restore again to finish.' }
      if (failure != '') { said = said + ' One lane could not be checked: ' + failure }
      dsx.variable.result = said
      dsx.event('noted', { message: said })
      dsx.event('restored', { coins: coins })
    </action>

    <style as="row" spacing="14" alignItems="flex-start" paddingV="12"/>
    <style as="label" fontSize="16" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="note" fontSize="13" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.45)" lineSpacing="3"/>
    <style as="btn" background="#26262C" radius="18" paddingH="16" paddingV="12" fontSize="13" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="compact" spacing="8" alignItems="stretch" paddingTop="4"/>
    <!-- the footer-link spelling (the Membership page): 12pt, a 32pt tap floor, the result
         line under it when there is one -->
    <style as="linkWrap" spacing="4" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="linkTxt" fontSize="12" fontWeight="semibold" color="#8E8E93" minHeight="32"/>
    <style as="linkNote" fontSize="12" color="#8E8E93" textAlign="center" lineSpacing="4"/>
    <style as="compactBtn" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" radius="20" paddingV="12" fontSize="14" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <!-- the nav-row PILL (spec §10: "Restore" top-right on /vip and /membership — 15pt on
         rgba(255,255,255,0.12), radius 8). The 44pt hit area is the wrapper; the pill is the
         visible part inside it, so the control meets the tap floor without a chunky chrome. -->
    <style as="pillWrap" minHeight="44" alignItems="center" spacing="0"/>
    <style as="pillTxt" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.12)" radius="8" paddingH="12" paddingV="7" fontSize="15" fontWeight="medium" color="#FFFFFF"/>
  </head>

  <!-- PILL — the nav-row control; outcomes go out as `noted`, the host prints them -->
  <hstack class="pillWrap" visible-if="dsx.attribute.variant == 'pill'" on:tap="dsx.action.tapped()"
          a11yGroup="true" a11yTrait="button" a11yLabel="Restore purchases made on this account">
    <text value="{{ dsx.variable.pillLabel }}" class="pillTxt"/>
  </hstack>

  <!-- CEILING — the disclosure alone, for a page whose control is the pill above: what this
       lane cannot reach is still said where the reader is (Article 7) -->
  <text value="{{ dsx.variable.ceiling }}" class="note" visible-if="dsx.attribute.variant == 'ceiling'"/>

  <!-- LINK — the footer spelling: one word beside Terms and Privacy (Membership) -->
  <vstack class="linkWrap" visible-if="dsx.attribute.variant == 'link'">
    <text value="{{ dsx.variable.signedIn ? 'Restore' : 'Sign in to restore' }}" class="linkTxt"
          on:tap="dsx.action.tapped()" a11yTrait="button" a11yLabel="Restore purchases made on this account"/>
    <text value="{{ dsx.variable.line }}" class="linkNote" visible-if="dsx.variable.result != '' || dsx.variable.busy"/>
  </vstack>

  <!-- ROW — the disclosure spelling, under a benefit list -->
  <hstack class="row" visible-if="dsx.attribute.variant != 'compact' &amp;&amp; dsx.attribute.variant != 'link' &amp;&amp; dsx.attribute.variant != 'pill' &amp;&amp; dsx.attribute.variant != 'ceiling'">
    <image icon="arrow.clockwise" iconSize="15" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.45)" a11yHidden="true"/>
    <vstack spacing="3" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0" alignItems="stretch">
      <text value="Restore purchases" class="label"/>
      <text value="{{ dsx.variable.line }}" class="note"/>
      <text value="{{ dsx.variable.ceiling }}" class="note"/>
    </vstack>
    <button label="{{ dsx.variable.btnLabel }}" class="btn" on:tap="dsx.action.tapped()"
            a11yLabel="Restore purchases made on this account"/>
  </hstack>

  <!-- COMPACT — the purchase-sheet spelling, where the price list is the point -->
  <vstack class="compact" visible-if="dsx.attribute.variant == 'compact'">
    <button label="{{ dsx.variable.signedIn ? 'Restore purchases' : 'Sign in to restore purchases' }}"
            class="compactBtn" on:tap="dsx.action.tapped()"/>
    <text value="{{ dsx.variable.line }}" class="note" visible-if="dsx.variable.result != '' || dsx.variable.busy"/>
  </vstack>
</vstack>
